Filters are where the gateway earns its keep. A `GlobalFilter` applies to every route; a `GatewayFilter` applies to specific routes. The most common production filters are JWT authentication, rate limiting, and circuit breaking.

A JWT authentication filter validates the token before the request reaches any backend service. This means your downstream services never see unauthenticated traffic — they can trust that if a request arrives, it has a valid identity attached.

```java
@Component
public class JwtAuthFilter implements GlobalFilter, Ordered {

    private final JwtUtil jwtUtil;

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange,
                             GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        String path = exchange.getRequest().getPath().value();
        if (path.startsWith("/api/public/")) {
            return chain.filter(exchange);  // skip auth for public endpoints
        }
        String token = exchange.getRequest()
            .getHeaders().getFirst("Authorization");
        if (token == null || !token.startsWith("Bearer ")) {
            exchange.getResponse().setStatusCode(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
            return exchange.getResponse().setComplete();
        }
        Claims claims = jwtUtil.validate(token.substring(7));
        // Forward user identity to downstream services
        ServerHttpRequest mutated = exchange.getRequest().mutate()
            .header("X-User-Id", claims.getSubject())
            .header("X-User-Roles", claims.get("roles", String.class))
            .build();
        return chain.filter(exchange.mutate().request(mutated).build());
    }

    @Override
    public int getOrder() { return -1; } // run before routing filters
}
```

Rate limiting and circuit breaking are configured as route-level filters. The rate limiter uses Redis to track request counts across gateway instances. The circuit breaker prevents a failing backend from blocking the gateway's event loop.

```yaml
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      routes:
        - id: order-service
          uri: lb://ORDER-SERVICE
          predicates:
            - Path=/api/orders/**
          filters:
            - name: RequestRateLimiter
              args:
                redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 10   # 10 req/sec steady state
                redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 20    # allow bursts up to 20
                key-resolver: "#{@userKeyResolver}"     # rate limit per user, not globally
            - name: CircuitBreaker
              args:
                name: orderCB
                fallbackUri: forward:/fallback/orders   # serve degraded response
```

The circuit breaker filter is critical for gateway resilience. Without it, a single slow backend (order service responding in 30 seconds instead of 200ms) ties up Netty's event loop threads. With 50 concurrent requests to the slow service, the gateway stops responding to all traffic — including healthy services. The circuit breaker trips after a failure threshold and returns the fallback immediately, protecting the gateway's ability to route to healthy backends.

A `GlobalFilter` for JWT validation inspects the `Authorization` header before forwarding. If the token is missing or invalid, it short-circuits the filter chain and returns 401 immediately — the downstream service never receives the unauthorized request.

```java
@Component
public class JwtAuthFilter implements GlobalFilter, Ordered {

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        String token = exchange.getRequest()
            .getHeaders().getFirst(HttpHeaders.AUTHORIZATION);

        if (token == null || !isValidJwt(token)) {
            exchange.getResponse().setStatusCode(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
            return exchange.getResponse().setComplete(); // stops the chain
        }

        // Add extracted claims as headers for downstream services
        ServerHttpRequest mutated = exchange.getRequest().mutate()
            .header("X-User-Id", extractUserId(token))
            .build();
        return chain.filter(exchange.mutate().request(mutated).build());
    }

    @Override
    public int getOrder() { return -1; } // run before all other filters
}
```

`isValidJwt()` must be **non-blocking**. JWT signature verification (using a public key cached in memory) is CPU-only — non-blocking by nature. If you instead validate by calling an auth service via JDBC or a synchronous `RestTemplate`, you block the Netty event-loop thread. That thread handles I/O for hundreds of concurrent connections. One blocking call per request means the event loop stalls, connection queues grow, and throughput collapses.

The fix for any I/O-dependent validation: return a `Mono` from the validation step and use reactive operators (`flatMap`, `switchIfEmpty`) to chain the filter logic. The Gateway stays non-blocking end-to-end, and the event loop thread is never held.
